---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import FAQ from '../components/FAQ.astro';

const site = 'https://poke-survivors.org';
const title = 'Complete Move Guide: Evolution & Attack Patterns | PokéSurvivor';
const description = 'Master all moves in PokéSurvivor. Learn how move evolution works, discover 21 attack patterns, and optimize your build for victory.';
const updated = '2026-10-19';
const gameVersion = '2026-10-13 update';

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'How many moves are in PokéSurvivor?',
    a: 'The official moves page lists 103 moves as of October 2026, with 97 capable of evolving. The roster has expanded through updates—the October 10 update alone added 12 new moves.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I evolve a move?',
    a: 'Upgrade a move to level 5, obtain the required stat card (Power, Vigor, Reflexes, Sprint, Synthesis, Armor, or Magnet), then select the golden evolution card when it appears. Example: Ember becomes Fire Blast with Power.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can all moves evolve?',
    a: 'No. Six buff moves don\'t evolve: Swords Dance, Nasty Plot, Agility, Harden, Dragon Dance, and Aqua Ring. The other 97 moves have evolution paths.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What are the golden evolution cards?',
    a: 'They\'re special upgrade cards that appear once you\'ve maxed out a move (level 5) and obtained its required stat card. Selecting the golden card evolves the move into its stronger form.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I hold more than 4 moves?',
    a: 'Yes, through the Fusion Bonds system introduced October 11, 2026. Defeat legendaries in rifts to earn Bonds, which let you fuse up to 5 moves into a single slot. See our <a href="/fusion-bonds/">Fusion Bonds guide</a>.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do evolved moves work with Fusion Bonds?',
    a: 'The system doesn\'t restrict move types, so evolved moves should work—though this isn\'t officially documented. Evolve your moves first, then fuse them for maximum power in your fusion slot.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which stat card does my move need?',
    a: 'Check the <a href="https://pokesurvivor.com/en/moves/" rel="noopener">official moves page</a> for specific requirements. Power is the most common (used by most offensive moves), followed by Reflexes and Sprint.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I reset a move\'s upgrades?',
    a: 'No. Move upgrades and levels are per-run only. Each new run starts fresh—you\'ll level up and evolve moves again from scratch.',
  },
];

const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@type': 'Article',
    headline: title,
    description,
    datePublished: '2026-10-19',
    dateModified: updated,
    author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'PokeSurvivor Fan Guide' },
    publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'PokeSurvivor Fan Guide' },
    inLanguage: 'en',
  },
  {
    '@type': 'HowTo',
    name: 'How to Evolve Moves in PokéSurvivor',
    description: 'Step-by-step guide to evolving moves from base forms to powerful versions',
    step: [
      {
        '@type': 'HowToStep',
        name: 'Max Out Your Move',
        text: 'Upgrade a move to level 5 by selecting its upgrade cards when leveling up.',
      },
      {
        '@type': 'HowToStep',
        name: 'Obtain the Required Stat Card',
        text: 'Draw the specific stat card the move requires: Power, Vigor, Reflexes, Sprint, Synthesis, Armor, or Magnet.',
      },
      {
        '@type': 'HowToStep',
        name: 'Select the Golden Evolution Card',
        text: 'A golden evolution card appears once requirements are met—choose it to evolve the move.',
      },
    ],
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  alternates={[
    { locale: 'en', href: '/moves/' },
    { locale: 'zh-Hant', href: '/zh/moves/' }
  ]}
  description={description}
  keywords="pokesurvivor moves, move evolution, pokesurvivor move list, best moves, ember evolution, move upgrade guide, attack patterns, move types, buff moves, fusion bonds"
  ogType="article"
  ogImage="/og-moves.jpg"
  ogImageAlt="Complete Move Guide for PokéSurvivor"
  breadcrumbs={[
    { name: 'Home', url: '/' },
    { name: 'Moves', url: '/moves/' }
  ]}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <div class="page-wrapper">
    <article class="main-content">
      <header class="article-header">
        <p class="section-label">Guide</p>
        <h1>Complete Move Guide: Evolution & Attack Patterns</h1>
        <p class="article-meta">
          Updated {updated} · Based on {gameVersion}
        </p>
      </header>


      <section>
        <h2>What are Moves in PokéSurvivor?</h2>
        <p>
          Moves are your attacks. Your Pokémon fires them automatically while you focus on movement.
          The game has grown significantly since launch. The official moves page lists
          <strong>103 moves as of October 2026</strong>, with <strong>97 capable of evolving</strong>
          into stronger versions. Earlier community counts cited 64 moves—the roster expanded through
          updates like the <a href="https://pokesurvivor.com/en/news/" rel="noopener">October 10, 2026
          addition</a> of 12 new moves.
        </p>
        <p>
          You start each run with one move. As you level up, you pick from three cards: learn a new
          move, upgrade an existing one, or boost your stats. You can hold <strong>4 moves at once</strong>,
          but only one fires at a time. Switch between them using 1–4, Q/E (PC) or by tapping the move
          icon (mobile).
        </p>
        <p>
          Your strategy: match moves to the situation. Switch to the move that's super effective against
          what's in front of you. <a href="/how-to-play/">Our how to play guide</a> covers the basics;
          this page covers evolution paths, attack patterns, and when to choose which moves.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>How Move Evolution Works</h2>

        <figure class="content-image">
          <img
            src="/images/content/moves-evolution-hero.svg"
            alt="Move evolution system showing Ember evolving into Fire Blast with Power stat card requirement"
            width="1200"
            height="675"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption>Move evolution process: Max out a move, get the required stat card, then evolve it</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <p>
          Move evolution transforms base moves into more powerful versions. Ember becomes Fire Blast.
          Bubble becomes Surf. The process has three steps, and missing any one of them delays your evolution.
        </p>

        <h3>The 3-Step Evolution Process</h3>

        <figure class="content-image">
          <img
            src="/images/content/move-evolution-detailed.svg"
            alt="Detailed move evolution path from Thunder Wave to Discharge showing XP requirements"
            width="800"
            height="500"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption>Example evolution: Thunder Wave branches into multiple paths based on usage pattern</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <div class="evolution-flow">
          <div class="flow-step">
            <div class="step-number">1</div>
            <div class="step-content">
              <h4>Max Out Your Move</h4>
              <p>Upgrade a move to <strong>level 5</strong> by selecting its upgrade cards when leveling up.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="flow-arrow">↓</div>
          <div class="flow-step">
            <div class="step-number">2</div>
            <div class="step-content">
              <h4>Obtain the Required Stat Card</h4>
              <p>Draw the specific stat card the move requires: Power, Vigor, Reflexes, Sprint, Synthesis, Armor, or Magnet.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="flow-arrow">↓</div>
          <div class="flow-step">
            <div class="step-number">3</div>
            <div class="step-content">
              <h4>Select the Golden Evolution Card</h4>
              <p>A golden evolution card appears once requirements are met—choose it to evolve the move.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p>
          Example: you're running Ember. You upgrade it to level 5. At some point during the run, you
          pick a Power card (Ember's requirement). Next time you level up, a golden "Fire Blast" card
          appears. Take it. Ember evolves into Fire Blast—same slot, stronger attack.
        </p>

        <h3>The 7 Stat Card Requirements</h3>
        <p>
          Every move that can evolve requires one of seven stat cards. These are the same cards you draw
          to boost your Pokémon's base stats. Taking them once during a run unlocks evolutions for any
          maxed-out moves that need them.
        </p>

        <div class="stat-cards-grid">
          <div class="stat-card">
            <h4>⚔️ Power</h4>
            <p>Most common requirement for offensive moves. Unlocks high-damage evolutions.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="stat-card">
            <h4>❤️ Vigor</h4>
            <p>Health and stamina-related evolutions. Sustain-focused moves.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="stat-card">
            <h4>⚡ Reflexes</h4>
            <p>Speed and multi-hit upgrades. Fast-attacking moves.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="stat-card">
            <h4>🏃 Sprint</h4>
            <p>Movement and cooldown improvements. Dash and mobility moves.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="stat-card">
            <h4>🌿 Synthesis</h4>
            <p>Healing, duration, and area enhancements. Support moves.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="stat-card">
            <h4>🛡️ Armor</h4>
            <p>Defensive quantity upgrades. Barrier and protective moves.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="stat-card">
            <h4>🧲 Magnet</h4>
            <p>Attraction and chain effects. Chain and bounce moves like Hex → Poltergeist.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p>
          Check the <a href="https://pokesurvivor.com/en/moves/" rel="noopener">official moves page</a>
          for which card each move requires. Power is by far the most common—most offensive moves evolve
          with Power.
        </p>

        <h3>What Evolution Grants</h3>
        <p>
          Evolution isn't just a name change. Evolved moves gain significant combat advantages. Based on
          the official moves page, typical evolution benefits include:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Damage multipliers</strong> — ×1.6 to ×1.9 damage increases</li>
          <li><strong>Additional projectiles</strong> — +1 to +6 extra shots</li>
          <li><strong>Increased pierce</strong> — +1 to +2 enemies hit per projectile</li>
          <li><strong>Larger area/radius</strong> — 35% to 50% coverage increases</li>
          <li><strong>Extra spins, bounces, or chain jumps</strong> — more targets hit</li>
          <li><strong>Faster firing/charging</strong> — shorter cooldowns</li>
          <li><strong>Full 360° coverage</strong> — melee moves hit all around you</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The exact bonuses vary by move. Fire Blast doesn't just hit harder than Ember—it fires more
          projectiles and pierces more enemies. Surf doesn't just damage more than Bubble—it covers a
          wider area and knocks back hordes. Evolution is always worth it.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Move Types and Attack Patterns</h2>

        <figure class="content-image">
          <img
            src="/images/content/type-effectiveness-chart.svg"
            alt="Type effectiveness chart showing super effective matchups for top offensive types"
            width="800"
            height="450"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption>Quick reference: Fighting and Ground types are super effective against the most types</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <p>
          The October 10, 2026 update added 8 new attack patterns, bringing the total to 21 distinct
          types. Understanding these patterns helps you build balanced loadouts—you want variety, not
          four projectiles that all do the same thing.
        </p>
        <p>
          Below are all 21 attack patterns, organized by behavior. Specific move names come from the
          official moves page; the grouping is ours.
        </p>

        <div class="pattern-groups">
          <div class="pattern-group">
            <h3>Basic Attack Patterns</h3>
            <div class="pattern-list">
              <div class="pattern-item">
                <h4>Melee</h4>
                <p>Close-range attacks in arcs. You swing or bite at enemies near you.</p>
                <p class="pattern-examples"><em>Examples:</em> Tackle, Karate Chop, Bite</p>
              </div>
              <div class="pattern-item">
                <h4>Projectile</h4>
                <p>Ranged attacks fired at enemies. Classic shooter-style moves.</p>
                <p class="pattern-examples"><em>Examples:</em> Ember, Water Gun, Thunder Shock</p>
              </div>
              <div class="pattern-item">
                <h4>Beam</h4>
                <p>Piercing linear attacks that cut through multiple enemies.</p>
                <p class="pattern-examples"><em>Examples:</em> Hyper Beam, Ice Beam, Psybeam</p>
              </div>
              <div class="pattern-item">
                <h4>Burst</h4>
                <p>Radial attacks spreading in all directions from the player.</p>
                <p class="pattern-examples"><em>Examples:</em> Hyper Voice, Heat Wave, Mud Shot</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="pattern-group">
            <h3>Area Control</h3>
            <div class="pattern-list">
              <div class="pattern-item">
                <h4>Aura</h4>
                <p>Constant damage fields around the player. Passive damage zones.</p>
                <p class="pattern-examples"><em>Examples:</em> Grass Knot, Discharge, Bug Buzz</p>
              </div>
              <div class="pattern-item">
                <h4>Orbit</h4>
                <p>Projectiles circling the player, hitting enemies that get close.</p>
                <p class="pattern-examples"><em>Examples:</em> Fire Spin, Petal Blizzard, Gust</p>
              </div>
              <div class="pattern-item">
                <h4>Vortex</h4>
                <p>Pull enemies toward center, grouping them for follow-up attacks.</p>
                <p class="pattern-examples"><em>Examples:</em> Whirlpool, Sand Tomb, Ominous Wind</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="pattern-group">
            <h3>Movement-Based</h3>
            <div class="pattern-list">
              <div class="pattern-item">
                <h4>Dash</h4>
                <p>Movement-based attacks. You dash through enemies, dealing damage.</p>
                <p class="pattern-examples"><em>Examples:</em> Quick Attack, Flame Charge, Aerial Ace</p>
              </div>
              <div class="pattern-item">
                <h4>Trail</h4>
                <p>Leave damaging zones while moving. Enemies walking into the trail take damage.</p>
                <p class="pattern-examples"><em>Examples:</em> Flame Wheel, Toxic</p>
              </div>
              <div class="pattern-item">
                <h4>Spin</h4>
                <p>Rotating area attacks. You spin, hitting everything around you.</p>
                <p class="pattern-examples"><em>Examples:</em> Rapid Spin, Rolling Kick, Outrage</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="pattern-group">
            <h3>Tactical Deployment</h3>
            <div class="pattern-list">
              <div class="pattern-item">
                <h4>Turret</h4>
                <p>Stationary entities that fire independently. Place them, they attack for you.</p>
                <p class="pattern-examples"><em>Examples:</em> Substitute, Will-O-Wisp</p>
              </div>
              <div class="pattern-item">
                <h4>Mines</h4>
                <p>Place traps that detonate when enemies walk over them.</p>
                <p class="pattern-examples"><em>Examples:</em> Toxic Spikes, Spikes, String Shot</p>
              </div>
              <div class="pattern-item">
                <h4>Meteors</h4>
                <p>Falling projectiles from above, raining down on enemies.</p>
                <p class="pattern-examples"><em>Examples:</em> Hail, Rock Slide, Meteor Mash</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="pattern-group">
            <h3>Target Tracking</h3>
            <div class="pattern-list">
              <div class="pattern-item">
                <h4>Homing</h4>
                <p>Auto-tracking projectiles that follow enemies. Fire and forget.</p>
                <p class="pattern-examples"><em>Examples:</em> Energy Ball, Ice Shard, Shadow Sneak</p>
              </div>
              <div class="pattern-item">
                <h4>Chain</h4>
                <p>Attacks jumping between targets, spreading damage across hordes.</p>
                <p class="pattern-examples"><em>Examples:</em> Thunderbolt, Hex</p>
              </div>
              <div class="pattern-item">
                <h4>Bounce</h4>
                <p>Projectiles bouncing between enemies before expiring.</p>
                <p class="pattern-examples"><em>Examples:</em> Seed Bomb, Electro Ball, Signal Beam</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="pattern-group">
            <h3>Control Effects</h3>
            <div class="pattern-list">
              <div class="pattern-item">
                <h4>Stun</h4>
                <p>Ground-marked attacks that paralyze or freeze enemies in place.</p>
                <p class="pattern-examples"><em>Examples:</em> Thunder Wave, Freeze-Dry, Rock Tomb</p>
              </div>
              <div class="pattern-item">
                <h4>Shove</h4>
                <p>Push enemies away, creating space and controlling positioning.</p>
                <p class="pattern-examples"><em>Examples:</em> Vacuum Wave, Whirlwind, Extrasensory</p>
              </div>
              <div class="pattern-item">
                <h4>Quake</h4>
                <p>Sequential wave attacks spreading outward from the player.</p>
                <p class="pattern-examples"><em>Examples:</em> Magnitude</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="pattern-group">
            <h3>Special Mechanics</h3>
            <div class="pattern-list">
              <div class="pattern-item">
                <h4>Charge</h4>
                <p>Delayed powerful attacks. Charge up, then release massive damage.</p>
                <p class="pattern-examples"><em>Examples:</em> Hyper Beam, Solar Beam</p>
              </div>
              <div class="pattern-item">
                <h4>Buff</h4>
                <p>No damage; permanently build stats while active. Six buff moves don't evolve.</p>
                <p class="pattern-examples"><em>Examples:</em> Swords Dance, Nasty Plot, Agility, Harden, Dragon Dance, Aqua Ring</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p>
          Pattern variety matters. If you run four Projectile moves, you're vulnerable to fast-moving
          enemies that dodge shots. Mix in a Melee or Aura move to cover close range. If you run four
          slow Charge moves, you'll deal huge burst damage but struggle against swarms. Add a fast
          Projectile or Chain move to clear hordes.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Move Categories by Type Effectiveness</h2>
        <p>
          PokéSurvivor follows the same type effectiveness system as the main Pokémon games. Each move
          belongs to one of 18 types (Fire, Water, Grass, Electric, etc.), and type matchups affect
          damage: super effective moves deal more damage, resisted moves deal less.
        </p>

        <h3>Key Examples</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Fire</strong> moves are super effective against Grass, Ice, Bug, Steel</li>
          <li><strong>Water</strong> moves are super effective against Fire, Ground, Rock</li>
          <li><strong>Electric</strong> moves are super effective against Water, Flying (but ineffective against Ground)</li>
          <li><strong>Grass</strong> moves are super effective against Water, Ground, Rock</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Strategy: Switch for Advantage</h3>
        <p>
          Your Pokémon attacks with one move at a time, so switch to the move that's super effective
          against what's in front of you. PC: press 1–4, Q/E. Mobile: tap a move icon.
        </p>
        <p>
          For the full type chart, check the
          <a href="https://pokesurvivor.com/en/guide/" rel="noopener">official guide</a>
          or any Pokémon type chart—the rules are the same.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Special Move Mechanics</h2>

        <h3>Buff Moves: The 6 That Don't Evolve</h3>
        <p>
          Six moves in PokéSurvivor deal no damage. Instead, they permanently build your stats while
          active. These are the buff moves:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Swords Dance</strong> — builds physical attack</li>
          <li><strong>Nasty Plot</strong> — builds special attack</li>
          <li><strong>Dragon Dance</strong> — builds physical attack (faster than Swords Dance)</li>
          <li><strong>Aqua Ring</strong> — regenerates health over time</li>
          <li><strong>Agility</strong> — builds speed</li>
          <li><strong>Harden</strong> — builds defense</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          <strong>Important:</strong> Buff moves don't evolve. They're among the six moves out of 103
          that have no evolution path. Once you hold two buff moves, the game stops offering more—you
          can't fill all four slots with buffs.
        </p>
        <p>
          The exact stat increases aren't publicly documented—buff moves build stats gradually while
          active, but percentages vary by move. They're most useful in long runs where you have time
          to stack stats before pressure spikes.
        </p>

        <h3>Conditional Damage: Hex</h3>
        <p>
          <strong>Hex</strong> (Ghost Chain) has unique conditional damage: it deals "much more damage
          if they are burned, poisoned or slowed," according to the official moves page. Hex evolves
          into Poltergeist with Magnet. If you're running status-effect moves like Toxic or Will-O-Wisp,
          Hex synergizes—apply the status, then chain Hex through the debuffed horde.
        </p>

        <h3>Fusion Bonds: Combining Moves</h3>
        <p>
          The October 11, 2026 update introduced Fusion Bonds—a system that lets you fuse multiple
          moves into a single slot. Defeat legendaries in <a href="/rifts/">rifts</a> to earn Bonds.
          Each Bond links one more move to your fusion slot. Up to 4 Bonds = 5 moves firing simultaneously
          from one slot.
        </p>

        <div class="disclaimer-box">
          💡 <strong>Fusion & Evolution Interaction:</strong> Evolved moves should work with Fusion
          Bonds—the system doesn't restrict move types—but this isn't officially documented. Evolve
          your moves first, then fuse them for maximum power in your fusion slot.
        </div>

        <p>
          Our <a href="/fusion-bonds/">Fusion Bonds guide</a> covers the full system: how to earn
          Bonds, which moves to fuse, and fusion strategies.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Best Moves by Situation</h2>
        <p>
          Move selection shifts as the run progresses. What works at minute 5 doesn't work at minute 25.
          Here's how to adapt.
        </p>

        <h3>Early Game (Minutes 0-10)</h3>
        <p>
          <strong>Priority:</strong> Evolve your starting move. Focus upgrade cards on it. Add one
          second move with a different attack pattern—if your starter is Projectile, grab a Melee or
          Burst move to cover close range.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Stat cards:</strong> Take the evolution requirement for your starting move at least
          once (usually Power). After that, prioritize damage upgrades. Defense can wait.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Avoid:</strong> Learning four new moves before evolving your first one. Upgrade depth
          beats breadth early.
        </p>

        <h3>Mid Game (Minutes 10-20)</h3>
        <p>
          <strong>Priority:</strong> Your starting move should be evolved by now. Add 2-3 more moves
          with type variety—cover Water, Fire, Grass, Electric so you're not walled by a single type.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Stat cards:</strong> Mix offense and defense. If a rift spawns and you're confident,
          take it—earning Bonds now sets you up for late-game power. See our <a href="/rifts/">rifts
          guide</a> for arena strategies.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Attack patterns:</strong> Aim for variety—one Chain or Homing move for hordes, one
          Beam or Projectile for single targets, one Aura or Orbit for passive damage while you dodge.
        </p>

        <h3>Late Game / Judgment Prep (Minutes 20-30+)</h3>
        <p>
          <strong>Priority:</strong> Shift from offense to survival. At minute 25, start favoring utility
          moves—healing, shields, dash moves. By minute 30, you enter
          <a href="/arceus-judgment/">Arceus Judgment mode</a>, where staying alive matters more than
          killing fast.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Recommended loadout for Judgment:</strong> At least two damage moves, fill the rest
          with utility—healing, shields, movement buffs. If you've collected Bonds, fuse your damage
          moves into one slot to free space for more utility.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Stat cards:</strong> HP (Vigor), Speed (Sprint), Defense (Armor) over raw damage.
          One mistake shouldn't end your run.
        </p>

        <h3>Boss Fights</h3>
        <p>
          Bosses spawn every 3 minutes (minutes 3, 6, 9, 12, 15, etc.). They're tanky, they telegraph
          four attack patterns, and they enrage at half HP.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Best move types for bosses:</strong> Beam, Projectile, Homing—single-target, high
          uptime moves. Avoid Melee (too risky to stay close) and slow Charge moves (you need consistent
          DPS, not burst).
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Strategy:</strong> Keep circling. Watch the ground markers. Switch to your highest-DPS
          single-target move. After the boss dies, switch back to horde-clearing moves.
        </p>

        <h3>Co-op Mode</h3>
        <p>
          Playing with friends? Move selection shifts slightly. See our <a href="/co-op/">co-op guide</a>
          for full multiplayer strategies. Key points:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Type coverage:</strong> Coordinate with your team. If someone's running Fire moves,
            you cover Water or Electric.</li>
          <li><strong>Support moves:</strong> One player running Aqua Ring or healing moves helps the
            team sustain.</li>
          <li><strong>Buff moves:</strong> Swords Dance and Nasty Plot benefit the whole team's damage
            if you're stacking stats together.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <FAQ heading="Common Questions" items={faq} />

      <aside class="sources" aria-labelledby="sources-title">
        <h2 id="sources-title">Sources and Updates</h2>
        <p>
